this footage shot by a tram enthusiast  gives you a drivers eye view of the  deadly Junction as the tram emerges from  the tunnel watch and listen as the  brakes are applied bringing the tram  speed right down to the designated 20  kilometers an hour limit  you can see the sign on the left of the  track  the Sandilands junction is negotiated  safely before the tram approaches the  station but on the morning of the 9th of  November it was a different story  with a devastating outcome an interim  report has found evidence the tram that  day was travelling much too fast the  findings have prompted calls for  automatic speed inhibitors to be fixed  to all trams but according to senior  crash investigators it's simply not  practical this is a tramway system and  the implementation of any new safety  system will be tight will take time and  needs to be considered and based on  solid evidence so no we cannot speculate  at this stage on the recommendations we  make but we give full consideration to  the design issues the tram set off just  before 6 o'clock in the morning from  Lloyd Park it was dark and raining  heavily going through the sand lands  tunnel the tram was traveling at 80  kilometers an hour well within the speed  limit however just before the curve at  the junction the speed limit drops to 20  kilometers an hour the tram dropped at  speed to just 70 kilometers an hour  still 3 times over the speed limit and  at 607 the tram derailed traveling 25  metres before it came to a stop  investigators so far have found no fault  in the track or mechanical failure of  the trams braking system but something  went seriously wrong leading to the  deaths of seven people one of the  important things that comes from the  report is that the signage was very  close to the actual bend itself I think  the report suggests is about 30 meters  or so in front of the bent it takes  something like under an 80 meter to stop  the train by the time it gets to that  sign so there's a clear need first of  all to improve the science so the  signage is further along the railway and  for the speed limit to be reduced much  further ahead as well the safety record  for trams is good but it's a relatively  new mass transport system commuters in  Croydon told us they have mixed feelings  about the network this is like a big  eye-opener for all drivers both tram  train buses that you need  be careful and a lot of lies are in your  hand the trams have got an excellent  history up to this point it seems like  an exceptional circumstance here it  hasn't rocked my confidence in them at  all really  the final report will take months to  complete investigators know what caused  the crash but desperately need to find  out why Ash's Joshi Sky News
 

